<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A shared receipt rule needs an identifier. Android has a familiar UUID API; the iOS compilation cannot use that JVM class. The rule can stay shared if its dependency has a contract that each target can satisfy.</p>

Follow source visibility, platform implementations and target compilation before evaluating compiler-generation claims.

<!-- @after The expect / actual Mechanism :: visual -->
### Two questions the build must answer

<figure class="viz"><table><caption>Source visibility is not a runtime hop</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>commonMain: receipt rule and identifier contract</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>androidMain: Android implementation</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>iosMain: Apple implementation</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Outputs: target artifacts, then application integration</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The boundaries in this example are different responsibilities, not implied packet transfers.</figcaption></figure>

A receipt identifier makes the source-set boundary concrete. The shared calculation can accept an identifier factory whose return type belongs in common code. Android can construct that factory using its available APIs, while the Apple composition root supplies its own implementation. The shared rule need not know either platform class. If expect/actual better fits a small platform primitive, its actual declaration must still be available to every target compilation that needs it.

The hierarchy controls which sources and APIs participate in each compilation. It does not mean commonMain executes first and then sends a request into iosMain. K2 changes compiler analysis; target outputs, platform API access and the consumer boundary remain separate questions. After changing the hierarchy, compile every affected target and inspect the exported consumer surface rather than treating a successful Android compilation as proof of an Apple integration.

<!-- @after Hierarchical Project Structure :: animation -->
### One contract, two target compilations

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">One contract, two target compilations</span><div class="stage viz-tree"><div class="node" data-el="n0" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Common source</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="row"><div class="node" data-el="n1" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Android target</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n2" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Apple target</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div></div><div class="node" data-el="n3" data-art="parcel" data-art-role="queue"><strong>Consumer</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Declare", "caption": "The shared calculation calls a platform-neutral contract. It cannot import java.util.UUID merely because Android is one target.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Common APIs only", "v1": "JVM APIs available here", "v2": "Apple APIs available here", "v3": "No binary yet"}}, {"label": "Supply", "caption": "Each target supplies its implementation through the applicable hierarchy, or receives an ordinary interface implementation at construction.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Same required behaviour", "v1": "Android implementation", "v2": "Apple implementation", "v3": "Contract remains small"}}, {"label": "Compile", "caption": "The configured target compilations analyse their visible sources and dependencies and use the appropriate backends.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Shared sources participate", "v1": "Android artifact", "v2": "Native artifact", "v3": "Different outputs"}}, {"label": "Integrate", "caption": "The apps consume the resulting API. Check the Swift-facing form and ownership separately from Kotlin source compilation.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Rule is shared", "v1": "Android caller", "v2": "Apple caller", "v3": "Integration must be tested"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/multiplatform/multiplatform-hierarchy.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Pitfalls :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Can commonMain import Foundation directly?</summary><p>Only a compatible narrower Apple source set can use that platform API; put an appropriate common contract at the seam.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does K2 make all targets one compilation?</summary><p>Target compilations still have distinct visibility, dependencies and backend work.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a matching actual prove product parity?</summary><p>It proves declaration matching. Behaviour, lifecycle and native consumer tests remain necessary.</p></details>

